// scrml.dev /reference — landing page for the reference section
//
// Filesystem-routed at /reference. Closes the 404 from the
// top-bar + sidebar nav links that pointed to /reference.
//
// Per architecture DD §6: Day-30 plan is full coverage of Tier 1 +
// Tier 2 elements / keywords / contexts. This landing page enumerates
// the categories + lists what's shipped so far vs queued.

<page>

    // REFERENCE SIDEBAR opt-in — static, page-scoped, zero JS. The nav is
    // authored once in app.scrml and hidden by default; this shows it and
    // marks THIS page's link active. See scripts/gen-reference-nav.mjs.
    #{
        /* !important is load-order, not laziness: the shell's app.css is linked
           AFTER this page's stylesheet, so its `.refnav{display:none}` default
           wins at equal specificity. */
        .ref-shell { display: grid !important; }
        .refnav { display: block !important; }
    }

    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-3xl">

        <header class="not-prose mb-8 pb-6 border-b border-slate-200">
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-500 mb-2">
                scrml.dev
            </div>
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
                Reference
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 mt-2">
                Every element, keyword, context, and error code in
                scrml. Reference pages summarize SPEC.md at MDN-rigor;
                SPEC.md is authoritative when they disagree.
            </p>
        </header>

        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // Elements
        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Elements
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Scrml-defined structural elements. NOT HTML &mdash; the
            grammar treats them as first-class language constructs.
            See SPEC &sect;4.15 + &sect;24.4 for the registry.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/engine" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;engine&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Tier 2 state machine. The canonical structural form for UI as a fully-handled state machine.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/match" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;match&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Tier 1 pattern matching. Structural exhaustiveness, no active rules; promotes mechanically to &lt;engine&gt;.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/errors" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;errors&gt;
                </a> &mdash; First-class element for rendering the auto-synthesized validity surface.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/program" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;program&gt;
                </a> &mdash; The root container. Owns route inference, middleware, channels, documentary metadata.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/channel" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;channel&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Real-time multi-client state. V5-strict body, auto-sync, no &#64;shared modifier.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/auth" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;auth&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Per-role visibility constraint. Drives the per-route per-role artifact splitter.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/onTransition" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;onTransition&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Engine effect element. Runs at transition commit; filterable by from / to / once / if.
            </li>

            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/onTimeout" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;onTimeout&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Per-state-child timer. Armed on entry, cleared on exit. Optional name= for cancellation.
            </li>

            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/onIdle" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;onIdle&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Engine-wide event-timeout watchdog. Armed at module-init, reset on every successful transition.
            </li>

            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/page" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;page&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Route declaration. Filesystem-routed; path= overrides. Per-route per-role artifacts.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/schema" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &lt;schema&gt;
                </a> &mdash; Declarative DB schema. SQL-mirror canonical plus additive shared-core validators; migration diff at compile time.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/onTimeout" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline"><span class="font-mono">&lt;onTimeout&gt;</span></a>,
                <a hard href="/reference/elements/onIdle" class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline"><span class="font-mono">&lt;onIdle&gt;</span></a>
                &mdash; engine temporal surface (per-state timeout + engine-wide idle watchdog).
            </li>
        </ul>

        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // Contexts
        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Contexts
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Braced regions that switch the grammar mode. Each context
            has its own dialect; the compiler dispatches by the opener.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/contexts/logic" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    &#36;&#123; ... &#125;
                </a> &mdash; Logic. The foundational context; reactive computation, control flow, inline writes.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/contexts/sql" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    ?&#123; ... &#125;
                </a> &mdash; SQL. Per-handler coalescing, N+1 loop hoist, multi-database adaptation.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/contexts/meta" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    ^&#123; ... &#125;
                </a> &mdash; Meta. Compiler-directed annotation on a declaration.
            </li>
            <li class="text-slate-500">
                <span class="font-mono">#&#123; ... &#125;</span> &mdash; CSS.
                <span class="font-mono">_&#123; ... &#125;</span> &mdash; foreign code.
                &mdash; not yet documented here.
            </li>
        </ul>

        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // Keywords (queued)
        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Keywords
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Reserved words with normative semantics. Four have their own
            reference page; the rest are documented in context on the
            pages that use them.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-1 text-slate-700 text-sm mb-3">
            <li>
                <a hard href="/reference/keywords/derived" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">derived</a>
                &middot;
                <a hard href="/reference/keywords/is-some" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">is some</a>
                &middot;
                <a hard href="/reference/keywords/lift" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">lift</a>
                &middot;
                <a hard href="/reference/keywords/req" class="font-mono text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">req</a>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <ul class="space-y-1 text-slate-700 font-mono text-sm">
            <li class="text-slate-500">
                fn, function, lin, pinned, is, not, &tilde;, reset, fail, default
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 text-sm">
            Two are deprecated and should not be reached for in new code:
            <span class="font-mono">pure</span> is superseded language-wide
            by <span class="font-mono">fn</span>, and the
            <span class="font-mono">server</span> modifier on a function is
            deprecated in favour of placement inference. (<span
            class="font-mono">server fn</span> is a different construct and
            is <em>not</em> deprecated &mdash; it stays, for server-pinned
            helpers.)
        </p>

        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // Error codes (queued)
        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Error codes
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Every error code the compiler can emit has a definition in
            SPEC &sect;34. <strong>54 codes have their own page here</strong>,
            grouped into families in the sidebar &mdash; each with the full
            message, when it fires, and a minimal repro. The normative
            catalog lives at
            <a href="https://github.com/bryanmaclee/scrml/blob/main/compiler/SPEC.md"
               class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                compiler/SPEC.md
            </a>
            section 34.
        </p>

        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // What this reference IS and ISN'T
        // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            How to read these pages
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <strong>Each feature page follows an 8-section template:</strong>
                summary, syntax, worked example, semantics + errors,
                edge cases, related features, availability, spec link.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Worked examples are real scrml.</strong> Examples
                target the current compiler and the canonical language shape;
                where a feature's runtime is still landing, the page says so.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>SPEC.md is the authority.</strong> When a
                reference page disagrees with SPEC.md, the spec wins.
                Surface the contradiction and we'll fix the docs.
            </li>
        </ul>

    </article>

</>
